WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.152
This slide shows four columns that could all be stored as integers.

00:00:04.152 --> 00:00:09.972
Heart rate is a measurement recorded to whole units.

00:00:09.972 --> 00:00:12.072
Emergency visits is a count.

00:00:12.072 --> 00:00:14.372
Patient ID is a label.

00:00:14.372 --> 00:00:19.157
Satisfaction is ordinal. The same technical dtype supports four different analytical meanings.

00:00:19.157 --> 00:00:24.282
This is why the correct question is not, what dtype does pandas show?

00:00:24.282 --> 00:00:30.487
The correct question is, how was this value generated and what comparisons make sense?

00:00:30.487 --> 00:00:37.577
Connect this idea to a hospital combining patient tables, device JSON, and clinical notes.

00:00:37.577 --> 00:00:43.057
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.057 --> 00:00:50.897
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:50.897 --> 00:00:57.042
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:57.042 --> 00:01:02.720
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
